{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Crusader nobleman who held significant influence during the 12th century in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Tripoli. He became Count of Tripoli in 1152 succeeding his brother Raymond II whose death left the county in a vulnerable position. Raymond III is best remembered for his prolonged conflict with the Muslim ruler Nur ad-Din who sought to expand his power in the Levant.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\nEarly Life and Rise to Power\nRaymond III was born around 1115 to Pons of Tripoli and his wife Cecilia of France. He belonged to the prominent House of Toulouse which had strong ties to both the French and Levantine nobility. Alongside his brother Raymond II he received a thorough education in chivalry and governance which prepared him for leadership. Raymond III’s early years were marked by political maneuvering as he navigated alliances with the Kingdom of Jerusalem led by Baldwin III and his regent Eleanor of Aquitaine. His ascension to the countship came after Raymond II’s death in 1152 which occurred during a battle against the forces of Nur ad-Din.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\nConflict with Nur ad-Din\nOne of Raymond III’s defining challenges was his rivalry with Nur ad-Din the powerful Zengid ruler who sought to reassert Muslim control over former Crusader territories. Unlike his predecessor Raymond II Raymond III adopted a more conciliatory approach initially trying to negotiate with Nur ad-Din. However these efforts failed as Nur ad-Din continued to pressure the Crusader states. The conflict escalated when Raymond III refused to pay tribute and allowed Nur ad-Din’s vassals to raid his lands. In 1164 Raymond III joined forces with Baldwin III King of Jerusalem to confront Nur ad-Din directly. Despite initial successes the coalition suffered a crushing defeat at the Battle of Harim in 1164 which forced Raymond III to retreat and seek assistance from Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Komnenos.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\nLater Years and Legacy\nAfter the setback at Harim Raymond III continued to play a key role in the political landscape of the Levant. He maintained alliances with the Byzantine Empire and even married Eudokia Komnene a niece of Emperor Manuel I in 1169 further solidifying ties. His later years were spent in a mix of diplomacy warfare and occasional cooperation with Muslim rulers attempting to preserve Tripoli’s independence. Raymond III’s reign ended in 1187 when he was captured by Saladin during the Siege of Tripoli. He was imprisoned until his death in 1189 leaving behind a complex legacy as a ruler who strove to balance diplomacy and resistance in the face of overwhelming Muslim power.\n\nRaymond III’s life reflects the broader struggles of the Crusader states in the 12th century where political acumen and military prowess were essential for survival. His dealings with Nur ad-Din and Saladin highlight the shifting dynamics of power in the region during this turbulent period. Despite his ultimate defeat Raymond III’s efforts contributed to the prolonged existence of the County of Tripoli as a Crusader stronghold.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06853077560663223}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00012003655137959868}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.006313735153526068}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.3385981023311615}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0004211969207972288}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.020603911951184273}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.4360619783401489}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.12084155529737473}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -1.2485990524291992}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -0.00012468514614738524}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.5645605325698853}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.10466960072517395}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -1.0008091926574707}, {"token": " held", "logprob"